==Old English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/treːow/|lang=ang}}

===Etymology 1===
From West {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|trewwō|lang=gem-pro}}.  Cognate with Middle Dutch {{term|trouwe|lang=dum}} (Dutch {{term|trouw|lang=nl}}), Old Saxon {{term|treuwa|lang=osx}}, Old High German {{term|triuwa|lang=goh}} (German {{term|Treue|lang=de}}).

====Noun====
{{ang-noun|g=f|head=trēow}}

# [[truth]], [[fidelity]]

=====Declension=====
{{ang-decl-noun-o-f|treow|alt1=trēow}}

=====Descendants=====
*English: [[truce]]

===Etymology 2===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|trewan|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|drew-o-|lang=ine-pro}}, a suffixed variant of {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|drew-|lang=ine-pro}}, {{recons|deru-||solid, steadfast|lang=ine-pro}}, whence probably also [[Proto-Indo-European]] noun for "tree" {{recons|dóru|lang=ine-pro}}.  Germanic cognates include Old Frisian {{term|tre|trē|lang=ofs}}, Old Saxon {{term|treo|lang=osx}}, Old Norse {{term|tré|lang=non}} (Danish {{term|træ|lang=nl}}), Gothic {{term|sc=Goth|𐍄𐍂𐌹𐌿|tr=triu|lang=got}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek {{term|δόρυ||wood, spear|tr=doru|lang=grc|sc=polytonic}}, {{term|δρῦς||tree, oak|tr=drus|lang=grc|sc=polytonic}}, Old Church Slavonic {{term|дрѣво|tr=drěvo|lang=cu|sc=Cyrs}} (Russian {{term|дерево|tr=dérevo|lang=ru|sc=Cyrl}}), Old Irish {{term|daur|lang=sga}} (Welsh {{term|derwen||oak|lang=cy}}).

====Noun====
{{ang-noun|g=n|head=trēow}}

# [[tree]]

=====Declension=====
{{ang-decl-noun-a-n|treow|alt1=trēow}}

=====Descendants=====
* English: [[tree]]

